// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  RSI DIVERGENCE PRO — 相對強弱背離（精準版）
//
//  TradingView's built-in RSI divergence, with the four holes closed that
//  Divergence_Radar_PRO already closed for MACD/KD/Fisher/CVD — and with the
//  SAME numbers the scanner uses, so the chart and the alerts cannot disagree.
//
//  ── WHAT THE BUILT-IN LEAVES OPEN ─────────────────────────────────────────
//  The stock rule is the textbook definition and nothing else:
//      bull = price lower-low AND rsi higher-low
//
//  1. NO NOISE FLOOR ON THE OSCILLATOR. An RSI higher-low of 0.01 points
//     satisfies the definition exactly as well as one of 12 points. This is
//     the biggest leak and the built-in has no control for it at all — it
//     filters the range between pivots (5…60 bars) and nothing else.
//     PRO requires the gap to clear `Min Momentum Gap`, expressed as a
//     FRACTION OF RSI's OWN RECENT RANGE rather than in RSI points, so the
//     same setting means the same thing on a quiet major and a violent alt.
//
//  2. NO SWING PROMINENCE. `ta.pivotlow(rsi, 5, 5)` calls any local dip a
//     pivot, including one inside a flat drift. PRO requires the PRICE pivot
//     to stand out from its neighbourhood by `Min Swing Size`, measured in ATR
//     AT THE PIVOT BAR — a swing that is not a swing cannot start a divergence.
//
//  3. DIVERGENCE ANYWHERE IN THE RANGE. A bullish divergence with RSI at 55 is
//     not exhaustion; it is noise wearing the shape. PRO can require RSI to
//     have been in its oversold/overbought zone AT THE PIVOT.
//
//  4. ONE PRIOR PIVOT ONLY. `ta.valuewhen(plFound, ..., 1)` compares against
//     the single previous pivot, whatever it happened to be.
//
//  ── WHAT IS *NOT* CLAIMED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Stricter is not the same as profitable. These filters make the signal rarer
//  and better-defined; they have NOT been shown to make money, on this chart
//  or anywhere else in this project. This repo has measured 48 high-win-rate
//  setups and found 46 of them losing. Treat a label as "this shape exists",
//  never as an entry.
//
//  ── WHY RSI AT ALL, WHEN FOUR SOURCES ALREADY EXIST ───────────────────────
//  Measured before it was added, on 25,454 bars across 22 crypto perps, as
//  Jaccard overlap of bullish-divergence bars:
//        KD  vs FISH  0.625   ← both ALREADY in the scanner
//        KD  vs RSI   0.340
//        MACD vs RSI  0.339
//        FISH vs RSI  0.277
//        CVD vs RSI   0.171
//  RSI is less redundant with the existing set than two members of that set
//  are with each other. That is the bar A/D failed when it was rejected from
//  the scanner's confluence (it is algebraically identical to CVD), and it is
//  a statement about INDEPENDENCE only — not about whether RSI predicts.
//
//  Mirrors app/strategy4.py: rsi_series() + bullish_divergence(). The defaults
//  below are pinned to that file by a parity test, so drift fails the suite.
//
//  ── 軋空 / SQUEEZE PANEL (added 2026-08-14) ────────────────────────────────
//  Asked: "shorts are being killed and price keeps ripping — can RSI see it?"
//  It cannot, and no price-derived oscillator can. RSI is computed from close
//  alone, so a rally driven by NEW BUYERS and a rally driven by SHORTS BEING
//  FORCED OUT are the same number to it. They are not the same event and they
//  do not behave the same afterwards.
//
//  Open interest is what separates them: price UP while OI FALLS means
//  positions are being CLOSED, which is the squeeze. Price up while OI RISES
//  is new money taking the long side. So OI is plotted here, on the RSI pane.
//
//  MEASURED before it was drawn — 30 crypto perps, 15m bars, forward 4h move
//  after a ≥3% 2h OI move:
//
//    state             n    mean     med   >+2%   >+5%  >+10%   <−5%
//    shorts_closing  207   +1.34   −0.23  35.3%  24.2%  19.3%  30.0%   ← squeeze
//    longs_opening   419   +1.92   +0.79  43.4%  29.6%  16.0%  18.9%
//    shorts_opening  312   −0.73   −0.55  33.0%  25.0%  12.5%  31.4%
//    longs_closing   257   −0.15   −0.73  36.6%  26.5%  16.0%  33.9%
//
//  The squeeze percentiles: p10 −13.6%, p50 −0.2%, p90 +20.3%, max +58.9%.
//
//  READ THAT CAREFULLY. The squeeze has the FATTEST TAIL of any state — 19.3%
//  of them run more than +10% — and its MEDIAN is slightly NEGATIVE, with 30%
//  falling more than 5%. It is a VOLATILITY condition, not a direction: the
//  typical squeeze fizzles and a minority go vertical. Its confidence interval
//  straddles zero, so it is not an edge.
//
//  The state that actually leans up is longs_opening — new money entering, not
//  shorts being killed. Its interval EXCLUDES zero and it has the lowest
//  downside rate of the four. That is the opposite of the intuition this panel
//  was requested to confirm, which is why it is written here rather than in a
//  commit message nobody reads.
//
//  ── THE LOTTERY-TICKET VERSION, MEASURED ──────────────────────────────────
//  "Ride the tail: tiny stop, far target, many attempts." Simulated PATH-
//  dependently — you must survive the stop to reach the tail, and a same-bar
//  tie goes to the stop — on 90 perps, entry at the NEXT bar's open, WITH
//  costs (taker 0.05% × 2 + funding).
//
//  軋空 shorts_closing, n=391 — every cell of an 11-cell grid:
//        3%/10%  −0.062R    3%/15%  −0.107R    5%/15%  −0.059R
//  ALL ELEVEN CELLS NEGATIVE. Profit factor 0.87–0.92. The fat tail is real —
//  19% ran +10% — and it does not pay for the 30% that stop out first. The
//  intervals straddle zero, so this is "no edge found", not "proven losing";
//  but eleven of eleven is a pattern, not one unlucky cell.
//
//  新多單 longs_opening, n=1148 — the SAME grid, same costs:
//        2%/20%  +0.311R *   3%/20%  +0.254R *   2%/15%  +0.229R *
//        3%/15%  +0.183R *   5%/20%  +0.183R *   3%/10%  +0.161R *
//  Nine of eleven cells have an interval EXCLUDING zero, and every cell is
//  positive. The lottery structure works — on the other state.
//
//  Caveat that limits all of it: 500 × 15m bars is 5.2 days, ONE regime, and
//  openInterestHist retains ~30 days so a real hold-out does not exist. Nine
//  significant cells out of eleven on five days is a strong hint, not a
//  validated strategy.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//@version=6
indicator("RSI Divergence PRO", shorttitle="RSI DIV PRO", overlay=false,
          format=format.price, precision=2, max_labels_count=200)

// ── RSI ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gRsi = "RSI"
rsiLen    = input.int(14, "RSI Length", minval=1, group=gRsi)
rsiSrc    = input.source(close, "Source", group=gRsi)

// ta.rma IS Wilder smoothing — (prev·(n−1) + new)/n — not an EMA. Swapping in
// ta.ema tracks closely enough to look right and differs by 2-4 points at the
// turns, which is exactly where a divergence is decided.
chg  = ta.change(rsiSrc)
up   = ta.rma(math.max(chg, 0), rsiLen)
down = ta.rma(-math.min(chg, 0), rsiLen)
rsi  = down == 0 ? 100 : up == 0 ? 0 : 100 - (100 / (1 + up / down))

// ── divergence filters (mirror of strategy4.py) ────────────────────────────
gDiv = "Divergence"
divLen    = input.int(5,  "Pivot Lookback (each side)", minval=1, group=gDiv,
     tooltip="A pivot needs this many bars EITHER side, so it is only confirmed this many bars later. Nothing here can be acted on at the pivot bar itself.")
rangeLo   = input.int(5,  "Min bars between pivots", minval=1, group=gDiv)
rangeHi   = input.int(60, "Max bars between pivots", minval=2, group=gDiv,
     tooltip="Two lows 400 bars apart are not one swing structure; comparing them is arithmetic, not analysis.")
minOscGap = input.float(0.05, "Min Momentum Gap (× RSI range)", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.01, group=gDiv,
     tooltip="How far RSI must have diverged, as a fraction of its own recent range. The built-in has no equivalent control — an 0.01-point higher low counts there exactly as much as a 12-point one.")
normLen   = input.int(200, "RSI range window", minval=20, group=gDiv)
minSwing  = input.float(0.8, "Min Swing Size (ATR at pivot)", minval=0.0, step=0.1, group=gDiv,
     tooltip="The PRICE pivot must stand out from its neighbourhood by this much ATR, measured at the pivot bar. A dip inside a flat drift is not a swing.")
useZone   = input.bool(false, "Require RSI extreme at the pivot", group=gDiv)
osLevel   = input.int(30, "Oversold", minval=1, maxval=50, group=gDiv)
obLevel   = input.int(70, "Overbought", minval=50, maxval=99, group=gDiv)

atrLen = input.int(14, "ATR Length", minval=1, group=gDiv)
atr    = ta.atr(atrLen)

// RSI's own range over `normLen`, so one gap setting means the same thing on a
// quiet major and a violent alt.
rsiRng = ta.highest(rsi, normLen) - ta.lowest(rsi, normLen)

ph = ta.pivothigh(high, divLen, divLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,  divLen, divLen)

// Values AT the pivot bar — divLen bars back, which is why this is confirmed
// late rather than repainting.
rsiPv = rsi[divLen]
atrPv = atr[divLen]
hiPv  = high[divLen]
loPv  = low[divLen]

// Swing prominence at the pivot bar.
legUp = hiPv - math.min(ta.lowest(low, divLen * 2 + 1)[0], loPv)
legDn = math.max(ta.highest(high, divLen * 2 + 1)[0], hiPv) - loPv
legOkH = atrPv > 0 and legUp >= minSwing * atrPv
legOkL = atrPv > 0 and legDn >= minSwing * atrPv

var float prevLowPrice = na
var float prevLowRsi   = na
var int   prevLowBar   = na
var float prevHiPrice  = na
var float prevHiRsi    = na
var int   prevHiBar    = na

bullDiv = false
bearDiv = false

if not na(pl)
    gap = na(prevLowBar) ? na : bar_index - divLen - prevLowBar
    zoneOk = not useZone or prevLowRsi <= osLevel or rsiPv <= osLevel
    if not na(prevLowBar) and gap >= rangeLo and gap <= rangeHi and legOkL and zoneOk
        priceLL = loPv < prevLowPrice
        rsiHL   = rsiPv > prevLowRsi
        bigEnough = rsiRng > 0 and (rsiPv - prevLowRsi) >= minOscGap * rsiRng
        bullDiv := priceLL and rsiHL and bigEnough
    prevLowPrice := loPv
    prevLowRsi   := rsiPv
    prevLowBar   := bar_index - divLen

if not na(ph)
    gapH = na(prevHiBar) ? na : bar_index - divLen - prevHiBar
    zoneOkH = not useZone or prevHiRsi >= obLevel or rsiPv >= obLevel
    if not na(prevHiBar) and gapH >= rangeLo and gapH <= rangeHi and legOkH and zoneOkH
        priceHH = hiPv > prevHiPrice
        rsiLH   = rsiPv < prevHiRsi
        bigEnoughH = rsiRng > 0 and (prevHiRsi - rsiPv) >= minOscGap * rsiRng
        bearDiv := priceHH and rsiLH and bigEnoughH
    prevHiPrice := hiPv
    prevHiRsi   := rsiPv
    prevHiBar   := bar_index - divLen

// ── 軋空 panel: open interest, which is where the squeeze actually shows ────
gSq = "Squeeze (OI)"
showSq  = input.bool(true,  "Show OI squeeze state", group=gSq)
sqSpan  = input.int(8,   "OI window (bars)", minval=2, group=gSq,
     tooltip="8 bars = 2h on a 15m chart, the same window the scanner ranks OI over.")
sqMinOi = input.float(3.0, "Min |OI change| %", minval=0.0, step=0.5, group=gSq,
     tooltip="Below this an OI move is noise. The measurement in the header used 3%.")

// TradingView publishes OI for perps as <EXCHANGE>:<TICKER>_OI. ignore_invalid
// keeps the indicator working on spot and stocks, where it simply goes blank
// rather than erroring.
oiTicker = syminfo.prefix + ":" + syminfo.ticker + "_OI"
oiRaw    = request.security(oiTicker, timeframe.period, close, ignore_invalid_symbol=true)
haveOi   = not na(oiRaw)

oiOk  = haveOi and not na(oiRaw[sqSpan]) and oiRaw[sqSpan] != 0
pxOk  = not na(close[sqSpan]) and close[sqSpan] != 0
oiPct = oiOk ? (oiRaw - oiRaw[sqSpan]) / math.abs(oiRaw[sqSpan]) * 100 : na
pxPct = pxOk ? (close - close[sqSpan]) / math.abs(close[sqSpan]) * 100 : na

// The same four-state read the scanner uses, so chart and alerts agree.
bigOi   = not na(oiPct) and math.abs(oiPct) >= sqMinOi
squeeze = bigOi and oiPct < 0 and pxPct > 0      // shorts closing  — 軋空
newLong = bigOi and oiPct > 0 and pxPct > 0      // longs opening
newShort= bigOi and oiPct > 0 and pxPct < 0      // shorts opening
longOut = bigOi and oiPct < 0 and pxPct < 0      // longs capitulating

// Background marks the CONDITION, never a direction. Amber, deliberately not
// green: the median squeeze is slightly negative and 30% of them fall >5%.
bgcolor(showSq and squeeze  ? color.new(color.orange, 86) : na, title="軋空 shorts closing")
bgcolor(showSq and newLong  ? color.new(color.green,  90) : na, title="新多單進場 longs opening")
bgcolor(showSq and newShort ? color.new(color.red,    92) : na, title="新空單進場 shorts opening")

var label sqLab = na
if showSq and barstate.islast and haveOi and not na(oiPct)
    txt = "OI " + str.tostring(oiPct, "#.##") + "% · 無明顯堆積"
    if squeeze
        txt := "軋空 · OI " + str.tostring(oiPct, "#.##") + "% 價 +" + str.tostring(pxPct, "#.##") + "%\n空單被平掉 —— 波動事件，不是方向"
    else if newLong
        txt := "新多單進場 · OI +" + str.tostring(oiPct, "#.##") + "%\n四種狀態裡唯一區間不含 0 的"
    else if newShort
        txt := "新空單進場 · OI +" + str.tostring(oiPct, "#.##") + "%"
    else if longOut
        txt := "多單平倉 · OI " + str.tostring(oiPct, "#.##") + "%"
    label.delete(sqLab)
    sqLab := label.new(bar_index, 5, txt, style=label.style_label_up,
                       color=color.new(color.black, 30), textcolor=color.white,
                       size=size.small, textalign=text.align_left)

// Fires on the CONDITION. The message says what it is and what it is not,
// because "軋空" reads as "buy" and the median one is slightly negative.
alertcondition(squeeze, "軋空 short squeeze (OI falling, price rising)",
     "軋空條件成立：未平倉下降、價格上漲 —— 空單正在被平掉。實測 207 次：19% 之後 4 小時漲超過 10%，但中位數 −0.2%、30% 跌超過 5%。這是「波動事件」不是方向，信賴區間包含 0。")

// ── smoothing MA (restored from the built-in) ──────────────────────────────
// The PRO rewrite dropped this and the zone fills, which left one bare line —
// the built-in's MA and its shaded bands are the parts that make an RSI pane
// readable at a glance, and they were removed for no reason beyond focusing on
// the divergence logic.
gMa = "Smoothing"
maType = input.string("SMA", "MA Type", group=gMa,
     options=["None", "SMA", "SMA + Bollinger Bands", "EMA", "SMMA (RMA)", "WMA", "VWMA"])
maLen  = input.int(14, "MA Length", minval=1, group=gMa)
bbMult = input.float(2.0, "BB StdDev", minval=0.001, maxval=50, step=0.5, group=gMa)

f_ma(src, len, t) =>
    switch t
        "SMA"                   => ta.sma(src, len)
        "SMA + Bollinger Bands" => ta.sma(src, len)
        "EMA"                   => ta.ema(src, len)
        "SMMA (RMA)"            => ta.rma(src, len)
        "WMA"                   => ta.wma(src, len)
        "VWMA"                  => ta.vwma(src, len)

isBB   = maType == "SMA + Bollinger Bands"
maOn   = maType != "None"
rsiMa  = maOn ? f_ma(rsi, maLen, maType) : na
bbDev  = isBB ? ta.stdev(rsi, maLen) * bbMult : na

// ── zones ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The RSI line is coloured BY ZONE rather than one flat purple, so "which zone
// am I in" is answerable without reading the axis: green in oversold, red in
// overbought, muted violet in the middle where RSI says least.
rsiCol = rsi >= obLevel ? #ff4d6d : rsi <= osLevel ? #2ee88a : #a78bfa
plot(rsi, "RSI", color=rsiCol, linewidth=2)

obBand = hline(obLevel, "Overbought", color=color.new(#ff4d6d, 45), linestyle=hline.style_dashed)
midBand = hline(50,     "Middle",     color=color.new(#787B86, 65), linestyle=hline.style_dotted)
osBand = hline(osLevel, "Oversold",   color=color.new(#2ee88a, 45), linestyle=hline.style_dashed)
topBand = hline(100, "Top", color=na, display=display.none)
botBand = hline(0,   "Bottom", color=na, display=display.none)

// Shaded zones: a tint between the bands so the middle reads as "nothing here",
// and solid-ish caps above 70 / below 30 that darken the further in it goes.
fill(obBand, osBand, color=color.new(#a78bfa, 94), title="中性區 band")
fill(obBand, topBand, color=color.new(#ff4d6d, 88), title="超買區 overbought")
fill(osBand, botBand, color=color.new(#2ee88a, 88), title="超賣區 oversold")

// Gradient caps — brightest at the extreme, so 85 looks different from 71.
rsiPlot  = plot(rsi, "RSI gradient anchor", color=na, display=display.none)
midPlot  = plot(50,  "mid anchor", color=na, display=display.none, editable=false)
fill(rsiPlot, midPlot, 100, obLevel,
     top_color=color.new(#ff4d6d, 25), bottom_color=color.new(#ff4d6d, 100), title="超買漸層")
fill(rsiPlot, midPlot, osLevel, 0,
     top_color=color.new(#2ee88a, 100), bottom_color=color.new(#2ee88a, 25), title="超賣漸層")

plot(rsiMa, "RSI-based MA", color=color.new(#ffd257, 0), linewidth=1,
     display=maOn ? display.all : display.none, editable=maOn)
bbU = plot(isBB ? rsiMa + bbDev : na, "BB Upper", color=color.new(#29ddff, 30),
           display=isBB ? display.all : display.none)
bbL = plot(isBB ? rsiMa - bbDev : na, "BB Lower", color=color.new(#29ddff, 30),
           display=isBB ? display.all : display.none)
fill(bbU, bbL, color=isBB ? color.new(#29ddff, 92) : na, title="BB fill")

// Which zone, in words, on the last bar — the fastest possible answer to the
// question this pane exists for.
// if/else rather than a stacked ternary: Pine treats a continuation line
// indented by a MULTIPLE OF FOUR as the start of a new block, so an aligned
// ternary chain compiles as CE10013 "expecting ':'". if/else has no
// continuation at all and cannot be broken by re-indenting.
zoneTxt = "中性偏空 " + str.tostring(rsi, "#.#")
if rsi >= obLevel
    zoneTxt := "超買 " + str.tostring(rsi, "#.#")
else if rsi <= osLevel
    zoneTxt := "超賣 " + str.tostring(rsi, "#.#")
else if rsi >= 50
    zoneTxt := "中性偏多 " + str.tostring(rsi, "#.#")
var label zoneLab = na
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(zoneLab)
    zoneLab := label.new(bar_index + 3, rsi, zoneTxt, style=label.style_label_left,
                         color=color.new(rsiCol, 20), textcolor=#0b1020,
                         size=size.small)

plotshape(bullDiv ? rsiPv : na, title="Bullish Divergence", offset=-divLen,
          location=location.absolute, style=shape.labelup, text="Bull",
          color=color.new(color.green, 0), textcolor=color.white, size=size.tiny)
plotshape(bearDiv ? rsiPv : na, title="Bearish Divergence", offset=-divLen,
          location=location.absolute, style=shape.labeldown, text="Bear",
          color=color.new(color.red, 0), textcolor=color.white, size=size.tiny)

// The alert says WHEN it was confirmed and WHERE the pivot was, because a
// label drawn `divLen` bars back reads as though it fired then. It did not.
alertcondition(bullDiv, "RSI Bullish Divergence (confirmed)",
     "RSI 多頭背離已確認 — 樞紐在 {{interval}} 的 5 根之前，現在才成立。這是形態，不是進場訊號。")
alertcondition(bearDiv, "RSI Bearish Divergence (confirmed)",
     "RSI 空頭背離已確認 — 樞紐在 {{interval}} 的 5 根之前，現在才成立。這是形態，不是進場訊號。")
